## 1. New bridge: strict `T5` -> nonlinear `S1`

Near uniform, label entropy has the exact Taylor form

\[
D(p\|u)=\frac1q\sum_j\sum_{m\ge2}
\frac{(-1)^m}{m(m-1)}x_j^m,
\qquad p_j=q^{-1}(1+x_j).
\]

The quadratic term is phase blind and protects all five strict doublet phases.
For modes `k=1,...,5`, the zero-sum cubic phase characters have exact rank four.
A primitive basis is

\[
2\phi_1-\phi_2,
\quad \phi_1+\phi_2-\phi_3,
\quad \phi_1+\phi_3-\phi_4,
\quad \phi_1+\phi_4-\phi_5.
\]

Thus ordinary cubic momentum-conserving resonances enforce

\[
\phi_k=k\alpha,
\]

reducing `T5` to the common carrier-translation `S1`.

This provides a structural explanation for the one-dimensional phase geometry
found earlier: it is the residual translation manifold after nonlinear
relative-phase locking.

## 2. Finite-q Umklapp hierarchy

With modes `1,...,5`, an order-`m` monomial has absolute wave-number sum at most
`5m`.  A nonzero reciprocal-lattice transfer is impossible for `5m<q`; at
`m=ceil(q/5)` one can explicitly sum `m` allowed positive frequencies to `q`.
Hence the first possible Umklapp order is exactly

\[
m_U=\lceil q/5\rceil.
\]

At `q=12`, cubic Umklapp makes the full cubic character rank five and pins the
remaining continuous shift to discrete `Z12`.  For larger q, relative phases
lock at cubic order while common-shift pinning moves to increasingly high
order: `5` at q=24, `10` at q=48, `20` at q=96.

This yields a perturbative hierarchy

\[
T^5 \xrightarrow{O(r^3)} S^1
\xrightarrow{O(r^{m_U})} Z_q.
\]

At any fixed finite Taylor order, large enough q removes all Umklapp at that
order.

## 3. Small-amplitude approximate T5 theorem

For `rho=q||f||_infinity<1`, Taylor remainder gives

\[
\left|D(u+f\|u)-\frac q2||f||_2^2\right|
\le
\frac{q^2}{6(1-\rho)^2}\sum_j|f_j|^3.
\]

Strict phase rotations preserve the quadratic term, so relative `T5` breaking
vanishes uniformly as the amplitude shrinks.  Deterministic phase scans give a
phase-spread exponent about `3.08`, consistent with cubic leading breaking.

This is an **approximate emergent HD symmetry**, not a stable HD phase: at the
origin phases are undefined and current radial dynamics does not keep all five
modal radii nonzero/soft.

## 4. Product-carrier theorem

For carrier `(Z_q)^d`, use low modes

`{e_i, 2e_i, e_i+e_j}`.

Cubic zero-sum relations

\[
2\phi_{e_i}=\phi_{2e_i},\qquad
\phi_{e_i}+\phi_{e_j}=\phi_{e_i+e_j}
\]

have exactly rank `m-d`; their composite-mode block is unimodular.  Therefore
all phases reduce exactly to

\[
\phi_k=k\cdot\alpha,
\qquad \alpha\in T^d.
\]

This works for every finite `d`; audits for `d=1,...,5` agree exactly.
For this low-mode set the first axial finite-q Umklapp appears only at
`ceil(q/2)`.

### Critical guardrail: configuration-space trap

The known composition of `M` cells already produces `(Z_12)^M` and can leave a
`T^M` phase manifold.  Here `M` is subsystem count, so the dimension grows
with system size.  That is naturally configuration space, not a fixed physical
base dimension.

The current strict 12-state operator is not secretly a nontrivial Kronecker
sum: it has nonzero coupling on every off-diagonal pair, whereas any
nontrivial `B tensor I + I tensor C` has forced cross-factor zeros.

Moreover, with only full permutation symmetry of otherwise identical cells,
the linear commutant is `span{I,J}`.  The only natural projector ranks are
`0,1,M-1,M`; no fixed `d=2,3,4` can be selected independently of `M`.

## Dimension-source verdict

